    "question": {
      ":question/id": "200085de-4d85-5544-9290-014ebbfe77b3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\sin\\left(44^\\circ\\right)\\sec\\left(22^\\circ\\right)\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Write $s=\\sin\\left(22^\\circ\\right)$ and $c=\\cos\\left(22^\\circ\\right)$. The **double-angle formula** gives $\\sin\\left(44^\\circ\\right)=2sc$.\n\n The reciprocal definition gives $\\sec\\left(22^\\circ\\right)=\\frac{1}{c}$.\n\nExpand the doubled sine and cancel the common factor.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2}2sc\\frac{1}{c} &=\\frac{2sc}{2c} \\\\ &=s \\\\ &=\\sin\\left(22^\\circ\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll cancellations use nonzero factors in the original expression.",
